TypeScript allows you to specify the type of a variable or property using type annotations. If you know that a value may have multiple possible types in theory, but in practice it will only have one specific type, you can use a technique called type narrowing or type assertions to provide additional information to the TypeScript compiler.

One way to achieve this is by using a type assertion, which essentially tells TypeScript to treat the value as a specific type, even if the type system cannot infer it from the code.

Here's an example:

```typescript
interface Animal {
  species: string;
}

interface Cat extends Animal {
  furColor: string;
}

interface Dog extends Animal {
  breed: string;
}

function getPet(): (Cat | Dog) | null;

// Assume that getPet() returns either a Cat or Dog instance, or null
const pet = getPet();

if (pet !== null) {
  // Narrow down the type of pet to either Cat or Dog
  const specificPet = pet as Cat | Dog;

  // Use specificPet with the properties of the actual type
  if ('furColor' in specificPet) {
    console.log(`My ${specificPet.species} has ${specificPet.furColor} fur.`);
  } else {
    console.log(`My ${specificPet.species} is a ${specificPet.breed}.`);
  }
}
```

In this example, the `getPet()` function returns either a `Cat` or `Dog` instance, or `null`. When you retrieve the pet value, you use a type assertion to narrow its type to either `Cat` or `Dog`, depending on the value of the `pet` variable.

You can then use the properties of the actual type in the `if` statement, since TypeScript knows the type of `specificPet` is either `Cat` or `Dog`.

Note that using type assertions can be a useful technique when you have a good understanding of your code's execution path, but it may lead to runtime errors if the assertion is incorrect. It's always a good practice to use type assertions sparingly and only when necessary, and to add appropriate comments or documentation to explain your reasoning.

